    <string name="format">Format</string>
    <string name="format_aztec_example">A train ticket holds its booking, its journey and its passenger in an Aztec code of a few hundred characters.</string>
    <string name="format_aztec_structure">A target of squares at its center, which lets a scanner find it, wrapped in layers of modules, from 15 × 15 to 151 × 151. It needs no blank margin around it.</string>
    <string name="format_aztec_use">Train and plane tickets.</string>
    <string name="format_codabar_example">`A40156B`: the number `40156` between `A` and `B`.</string>
    <string name="format_codabar_structure">Digits and six signs (- $ : / . +), framed by a start letter and a stop letter, A, B, C or D.</string>
    <string name="format_codabar_use">Libraries, blood banks and parcel services.</string>
    <string name="format_code_128_example">The parcel number `123456` is written in 3 characters of the digit set.</string>
    <string name="format_code_128_structure">Each character is 11 modules of 3 bars and 3 spaces. Three sets of characters cover the capitals, the small letters and the digits, the last one writing two digits a character. A check character comes before the stop pattern.</string>
    <string name="format_code_128_use">Shipping labels and logistics.</string>
    <string name="format_code_39_example">`SCANNY` is written `*SCANNY*`, between its two asterisks.</string>
    <string name="format_code_39_structure">Capital letters, digits and a few signs. Each character is 9 bars and spaces, 3 of them wide, hence its other name, Code 3 of 9. An asterisk opens the code and another closes it.</string>
    <string name="format_code_39_use">Industry, logistics and badges.</string>
    <string name="format_code_93_example">`SCANNY` reads as in Code 39, on a shorter width, with its two check characters before the end.</string>
    <string name="format_code_93_structure">The characters of the Code 39, each in 9 modules of 3 bars and 3 spaces, followed by two check characters: denser than the Code 39, and safer.</string>
    <string name="format_code_93_use">Logistics and postal services.</string>
    <string name="format_data_matrix_example">A serial number of 10 digits fits in a square of 12 × 12 modules.</string>
    <string name="format_data_matrix_structure">A square or a rectangle of modules, from 10 × 10 to 144 × 144. A solid L on two sides lets a scanner find it, a dotted line on the two others gives its size.</string>
    <string name="format_data_matrix_use">Printed tiny on electronic parts, medicines and mail.</string>
    <string name="format_ean_13_example">`4006381333931`: `400` for Germany, `6381` for the company, `33393` for the product, `1` for the check.</string>
    <string name="format_ean_13_structure">13 digits: the prefix of the country where the company is registered, the number of the company, the number of the product, then a check digit computed from the other 12, which tells a misreading.</string>
    <string name="format_ean_13_use">The products sold almost everywhere in the world.</string>
    <string name="format_ean_8_example">`96385074`: `963` for the prefix, `8507` for the product, `4` for the check.</string>
    <string name="format_ean_8_structure">8 digits: a prefix, the number of the product, given whole by the GS1 organization, then a check digit.</string>
    <string name="format_ean_8_use">The small products with no room for an EAN-13.</string>
    <string name="format_example">Example</string>
    <string name="format_itf_example">`15400141288763`, on a carton: `1` for the packing, `540014128876` for the product it holds, `3` for the check.</string>
    <string name="format_itf_structure">Digits in pairs, interleaved: the first in the bars, the second in the spaces between them. Each digit is 5 elements, 2 of them wide, hence its name, Interleaved 2 of 5.</string>
    <string name="format_itf_use">Cartons and pallets, as the ITF-14.</string>
    <string name="format_pdf417_example">The back of an American driving licence holds the name, the address and the birth date of its holder in a PDF417.</string>
    <string name="format_pdf417_structure">Stacked rows, from 3 to 90, of code words. Each code word is 4 bars and 4 spaces over 17 modules, hence its name. A start pattern and a stop pattern frame each row.</string>
    <string name="format_pdf417_use">Identity cards, driving licences and boarding passes.</string>
    <string name="format_qr_code_example">`https://scanny.app`, 18 characters, fits in a square of 25 × 25 modules at the lowest correction.</string>
    <string name="format_qr_code_structure">A square of black and white modules, from 21 × 21 to 177 × 177. Three squares in its corners let a scanner find it and turn it the right way. The content is written in binary, partly repeated by its error correction, so the code still reads when damaged.</string>
    <string name="format_qr_code_use">The most common code: links, payments, menus, tickets, Wi-Fi networks.</string>
    <string name="format_read_and_created">Scanny reads it and creates it.</string>
    <string name="format_structure">How it is made</string>
    <string name="format_upc_a_example">`036000291452`: `0` for the system, `36000` for the company, `29145` for the product, `2` for the check.</string>
    <string name="format_upc_a_structure">12 digits: a number system, 0 for most products, 5 digits for the company, 5 for the product, then a check digit. It is an EAN-13 that starts with 0.</string>
    <string name="format_upc_a_use">The products sold in North America.</string>
    <string name="format_upc_e_example">`04252614` is the short form of the UPC-A `042100005264`.</string>
    <string name="format_upc_e_structure">A UPC-A shortened to 8 digits by leaving out its zeros: the number system, 6 digits that hold the company and the product, then the check digit.</string>
    <string name="format_upc_e_use">The small products sold in North America.</string>
    <string name="format_use">Its use</string>
